### Join a poll
# Authenticated variant: POST {{host}}/sessions/{{sessionId}}/users/authed (Cognito JWT).
# On the authed variant, name is populated from the Google account's name claim instead of null,
# and the new user is linked to that Google account (googleSub, not returned in the response).

POST {{host}}/sessions/{{sessionId}}/users

# Response 201:
# {
#   "userId": "clever-fox",
#   "name": null,
#   "expiration": 1725453600
# }
#
# Response 400: max users reached (message includes limit)
# Response 404: session not found or expired

###

### Update user info (name)
# JSON Patch format. Allowed ops: replace.
# Allowed paths: /name
# Authenticated variant: PATCH {{host}}/sessions/{{sessionId}}/users/{{userId}}/authed (Cognito JWT).
# On the authed variant, if the user isn't already linked to a Google account, this call links it
# (googleSub set from the JWT) as a side effect — even though the request body only touches /name.
# If the user is already linked to a DIFFERENT account, the authed variant answers 403. That is a
# signal, not a wall: the unauthenticated route below patches any participant, because a poll link is
# the only credential a voter has. It exists so a caller can find out that the participant it is
# acting as belongs to somebody else — the fact the calendar routes refuse it for, and the one thing
# GET /users cannot tell it, since googleSub is stripped from every response.

PATCH {{host}}/sessions/{{sessionId}}/users/{{userId}}
Content-Type: application/json

[
  { "op": "replace", "path": "/name", "value": "Alice" }
]

# Response 200: updated user object
# Response 400: disallowed op, disallowed path, name > 50 chars
# Response 403: authed variant only — the participant is linked to a different Google account
# Response 404: session or user not found

###

### Get availability
# free is keyed [dateIndex][slotIndex] -- slotIndex is always 0 when the poll's usesTimes is false.
# Each date's row length matches slots[dateIndex] from GET /sessions/{sessionId} (see that endpoint):
# a date covered by a time-window override can have a different number of slots than other dates in
# the same poll, so free's rows are not guaranteed uniform width. There is no recurring pattern
# concept beyond overrides; every date/slot is otherwise independent.
#
# The stored record also holds calendarCheckedAt. Both this route and PATCH .../availability strip it
# on purpose: they are unauthenticated and take the participant in the path, so returning it would tell
# anyone holding the poll link which participants connected a calendar. Its absence here is deliberate,
# not an oversight -- read it from the availability POST .../calendar/sync returns to you instead.
GET {{host}}/sessions/{{sessionId}}/users/{{userId}}/availability

# Response 200:
# {
#   "userId": "brave-tiger",
#   "free": [
#     [false, false, false],
#     [true, true, false],
#     [true, true, true]
#   ],
#   "expiration": 1725453600
# }
#
# Response 404: session or user not found

###

### Set availability
# cells: array of { dateIndex, slotIndex, value }. slotIndex is always 0 when the poll's usesTimes is false.
# Valid slotIndex range depends on the target date: derive it from slots[dateIndex].length (GET
# /sessions/{sessionId}), not a single shared count -- an out-of-range slotIndex for that date's own
# window returns 400 even if it would be in range for a different date in the same poll.
PATCH {{host}}/sessions/{{sessionId}}/users/{{userId}}/availability
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "cells": [{ "dateIndex": 0, "slotIndex": 0, "value": true }]
}

# Response 200: the full updated availability record, same shape as GET .../availability
# Response 400: invalid cells (out-of-bounds dateIndex/slotIndex, non-boolean value)
# Response 404: session or user not found

###

### Get overlap grid and recommended meeting times
# Always reflects the whole poll -- there is no pattern/week view distinction anymore.
# recommendedMeetings counts only what each participant marked. Reading the overlap never reads anyone's
# Google Calendar: a calendar check is an explicit write (POST .../calendar/sync) that marks the person
# busy on their own grid, so by the time overlap runs, that busy time is already part of their marked
# availability. A participant who marked a slot free is free for it, whatever their calendar says.
#
# grid.cells is per-date (cells[dateIndex]), same order as the poll's dates -- and, like
# GET /sessions/{sessionId}'s slots, each date's row can be a different length when the poll has
# overrides (a date covered by an override group has as many cells as that group's window produces,
# not necessarily the same count as other dates). Each cell is self-describing (dateIndex, slotIndex,
# startMinute, endMinute), so iterate cells[dateIndex] directly rather than assuming a uniform width.

GET {{host}}/sessions/{{sessionId}}/overlap

# Response 200:
# {
#   "grid": {
#     "cells": [
#       [
#         { "dateIndex": 0, "slotIndex": 0, "startMinute": 540, "endMinute": 600, "freeCount": 1, "freeUserIds": ["brave-tiger"] }
#       ]
#     ],
#     "bestSlot": { "dateIndex": 0, "slotIndex": 0, "freeCount": 1, "freeUserIds": ["brave-tiger"] }
#   },
#   "recommendedMeetings": [
#     {
#       "dateIndex": 0,
#       "slotIndex": 0,
#       "date": "2025-09-04",
#       "startMinute": 540,
#       "endMinute": 600,
#       "freeCount": 1,
#       "freeUserIds": ["brave-tiger"]
#     }
#   ]
# }
#
# Response 404: session not found or expired

###

### Start Google Calendar connect flow
POST {{host}}/sessions/{{sessionId}}/users/{{userId}}/calendar/connect
Authorization: Bearer {{cognitoIdToken}}

# Response 200 (not yet connected): { "alreadyConnected": false, "authUrl": "https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth?..." }
# Response 200 (already connected, any poll): { "alreadyConnected": true }
# Response 400: not authenticated with Google
# Response 403: {userId} is someone else's participant, or has never linked a Google account. Sign in
#   and claim the participant first (POST /users/authed or PATCH .../authed), then connect.
# Response 404: session or user not found

###

### Check the calendar and mark booked hours busy
# Reads the signed-in person's Google Calendar and marks them busy on their own availability for this
# poll wherever the calendar says they are booked. It only ever marks busy: an hour the calendar
# reports free is left exactly as they set it, and an hour already marked busy stays busy and is not
# counted. Nothing records which hours came from the calendar, so a marked hour is indistinguishable
# from one they unmarked by hand -- and they can mark it free again with a normal PATCH .../availability.
#
# force (optional, default false):
# - false -- run only if this poll has never been checked for this person. If it has, the call is a
#   no-op: it returns applied: false, markedBusyCount: 0, the unchanged availability, and never calls
#   Google. This is the safe call to make on page load; without it a background check would silently
#   undo a deliberate "I'm free then" edit with nothing on the record to explain why.
# - true -- always check, bypassing both the once-per-poll rule and the cached-busy-data freshness
#   window. This is the "Check again" button.
#
# markedBusyCount is how many slots this call changed from free to busy -- 0 is a normal answer, not a
# failure. lastSyncedAt is epoch seconds of the last time this account's calendar was CHECKED, not the
# last time a check succeeded: a failed fetch moves it forward too. Do not present it as "your
# calendar was up to date as of X".
# availability is the full updated record (including calendarCheckedAt), so repaint the grid from it
# rather than issuing a second read.

POST {{host}}/sessions/{{sessionId}}/users/{{userId}}/calendar/sync
Authorization: Bearer {{cognitoIdToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "force": false
}

# Response 200 (checked):
# {
#   "applied": true,
#   "markedBusyCount": 4,
#   "lastSyncedAt": 1754006400,
#   "availability": {
#     "userId": "brave-tiger",
#     "free": [
#       [false, false, false],
#       [true, true, false],
#       [true, true, true]
#     ],
#     "calendarCheckedAt": 1754006400,
#     "expiration": 1725453600
#   }
# }
#
# Response 200 (already checked, force false): same shape with "applied": false, "markedBusyCount": 0,
# and the availability unchanged.
#
# Response 400: not authenticated with Google, or no calendar connected for this account
# Response 403: {userId} is someone else's participant, or has never linked a Google account. You can
#   only check your own calendar onto your own availability, so nothing about another participant --
#   including whether they ever connected a calendar -- comes back here.
# Response 404: session, user, or availability record not found
# Response 502: Google could not be reached -- availability is left untouched, so retrying is safe

###

### Google Calendar OAuth callback (browser redirect target, not called directly)
GET {{host}}/oauth/google-calendar/callback?code=...&state=...

# Always redirects (302) to {webAppUrl}/calendar-connected?status=connected|declined|error

###

### Get calendar connection state
# A calendar connection belongs to a Google account, not to a poll, so this route takes no poll and
# answers for whoever the JWT names. status is "not_connected" (no calendar linked, or never linked),
# "connected", or "error" (the last attempt to read Google failed -- offer a reconnect). lastSyncedAt
# is epoch seconds of the last check ATTEMPT, or null if this account has never been checked. A failed
# check moves it forward, so read it together with status: it dates the attempt, not the data. When
# status is "error" the busy data behind it is older than lastSyncedAt says.
#
# There is no "has this poll been checked" field here. That lives on calendarCheckedAt in the record
# POST .../calendar/sync returns, which the client already has.

GET {{host}}/calendar
Authorization: Bearer {{cognitoIdToken}}

# Response 200: { "status": "connected", "lastSyncedAt": 1754006400 }
# Response 200 (never connected): { "status": "not_connected", "lastSyncedAt": null }
# Response 400: not authenticated with Google

###

### Disconnect Google Calendar
# Revokes the connection for the whole Google account, so it takes no poll: one person's calendar is
# linked once and reaches every poll they are in. Availability already marked busy by a past check
# stays as it is -- disconnecting stops future checks, it does not undo past ones.

DELETE {{host}}/calendar
Authorization: Bearer {{cognitoIdToken}}

# Response 204: disconnected (or was never connected)
# Response 400: not authenticated with Google
